/-! # Delta invariant and normalization branches of an ordinary point

Source: Temkin, Stable Modification of Relative Curves, Appendix B, printed pp. 56–57;
Stacks Project, Curves, Tags 0C1U–0C1X; Varieties, delta-number-branches-inequality.
(blueprint: lem-trf-ordinary-point-delta-branches).
-/

@[expose] public section

set_option autoImplicit false
set_option relaxedAutoImplicit false

universe u

open CategoryTheory

namespace AlgebraicGeometry

/-- The normalization quotient at `x`, viewed as a k-vector space via the structural
scalar map. This uses the existing canonical normalization cokernel and stalk functor.
Stalks preserve cokernels, so this is precisely `(ν_* O)_x / O_x`, with the natural map.
Source: Temkin, Appendix B, definition of g(x), printed pp. 56–57;
Stacks Project, Varieties, Tag 0C1R.
(blueprint: lem-trf-ordinary-point-delta-branches). -/
noncomputable def normalizationDeltaModule {k : Type u} [Field k] {X : Scheme.{u}}
    (p : X ⟶ Spec (.of k)) (x : X) : ModuleCat.{u} k :=
  (ModuleCat.restrictScalars (Orgogozo.localScalar p x)).obj
    ((RingedSpace.moduleStalkFunctor X.toRingedSpace x).obj (conductorCokernel X))

/-- Finiteness, the branch–delta inequality, its ordinary-point equality criterion, and
its smooth and nodal special cases. A closed point over the algebraically closed field
is a k-rational point, so no choice of a rational lift is required. The normalization
is the canonical disjoint union of the integral component normalizations. The quotient
is the stalk of the canonical cokernel, with the structural k-action.

Source: Temkin, Appendix B, preceding Lemma B.1, printed pp. 56–57;
Stacks Project, Curves, Tags 0C1U–0C1X.
(blueprint: lem-trf-ordinary-point-delta-branches).

Proof, following the eleven steps of the DAG:
1. Restrict to an affine neighbourhood. Finite surjective normalization gives a finite
   nonempty fibre. Its points are closed and have residue field k. Finite type gives a
   reduced Noetherian local ring A of dimension one at x.
2. The normalization quotient theorem gives B equal to the integral closure of A and
   B/A of finite length at nonsmooth points. At smooth points regularity and normality
   identify B with A, so the quotient is zero.
3. A simple filtration has quotients k, hence B/A is finite dimensional. Curve
   normalization/completion and finite-extension completion identify its dimension
   with dim_k(C/H), for H the completion of A and C the completed normalization.
4. Finite-algebra completion decomposes C into completed local rings of the branches.
   Normalization smoothness and the smooth-curve local-ring theorem make these DVRs;
   DVR completion preserves this, and their structural residue fields are k.
5. Choose a uniformizer in each complete DVR. Completion modulo the maximal ideal
   and adic evaluation give a bijective power-series map by successive coefficient
   expansion. Uniqueness follows from the first nonzero coefficient and valuation.
6. Thus C is the product D of b copies of k[[t]]. Its residue map to k^b sends H onto
   the diagonal k. The surjection D/H to k^b/diagonal k gives delta ≥ b-1, with equality
   precisely when H is the equal-constant-term subring P.
7. Evaluate multivariate series on each coordinate axis. The image is P and the kernel
   is generated by mixed products: group every mixed monomial by its first two positive
   indices. This gives P equal to the coordinate-axes quotient, including b=1.
8. Coordinate idempotents make D finite integral over P. Inverting the tuple of
   uniformizers identifies their total fraction rings. Normality of the power-series
   factors shows that D is exactly the integral closure of P and D/P has dimension b-1.
9. An isomorphism H with the axes quotient identifies their total fraction rings and
   integral closures. Consequently C/H has dimension b-1, proving the converse.
10. Smoothness makes the quotient zero, forcing b=1. Conversely the one-axis completion
    is regular; completion regularity descends, and regularity with residue field k
    implies smoothness by the separable-residue smoothness criterion.
11. A node has two completed normalization factors and a simple normalization quotient,
    so b=2 and delta=1. The equality criterion gives the scalar-compatible two-axis
    presentation. Conversely that presentation is the ordinary-node definition.
-/
theorem ordinary_point_delta_branches
    (k : Type u) [Field k] [IsAlgClosed k] (X : Scheme.{u})
    [X.Over (Spec (.of k))] [IsReduced X]
    [LocallyOfFiniteType (X ↘ Spec (.of k))] [QuasiCompact (X ↘ Spec (.of k))]
    (hpure : X.IsPureDimension 1) (x : X) (hx : IsClosed ({x} : Set X)) :
    Finite {y : X.reducedNormalization // X.fromReducedNormalization y = x} ∧
    Module.Finite k (normalizationDeltaModule (X ↘ Spec (.of k)) x) ∧
    1 ≤ Nat.card {y : X.reducedNormalization // X.fromReducedNormalization y = x} ∧
    Nat.card {y : X.reducedNormalization // X.fromReducedNormalization y = x} - 1 ≤
      Module.finrank k (normalizationDeltaModule (X ↘ Spec (.of k)) x) ∧
    (Module.finrank k (normalizationDeltaModule (X ↘ Spec (.of k)) x) =
        Nat.card {y : X.reducedNormalization // X.fromReducedNormalization y = x} - 1 ↔
      IsOrdinaryMultiplePoint (X ↘ Spec (.of k)) x
        (Nat.card {y : X.reducedNormalization // X.fromReducedNormalization y = x})) ∧
    (IsOrdinaryMultiplePoint (X ↘ Spec (.of k)) x 1 ↔
      ∃ U : X.Opens, x ∈ U ∧ Smooth (U.ι ≫ (X ↘ Spec (.of k)))) ∧
    (IsOrdinaryMultiplePoint (X ↘ Spec (.of k)) x 2 ↔
      Orgogozo.IsOrdinaryDoublePoint (X ↘ Spec (.of k)) x) ∧
    ((∃ U : X.Opens, x ∈ U ∧ Smooth (U.ι ≫ (X ↘ Spec (.of k)))) →
      Module.finrank k (normalizationDeltaModule (X ↘ Spec (.of k)) x) = 0) ∧
    (Orgogozo.IsOrdinaryDoublePoint (X ↘ Spec (.of k)) x →
      Nat.card {y : X.reducedNormalization // X.fromReducedNormalization y = x} = 2 ∧
      Module.finrank k (normalizationDeltaModule (X ↘ Spec (.of k)) x) = 1) := by
  sorry

end AlgebraicGeometry
